Link checking as a product (2026-09-10)

Prompted by drlinkcheck.com. They sell broken-link checking alone at
$13-159/month: Lite free (2 sites, 1,500 links, one-time), Standard $13
(5/10,000, monthly+biweekly), Professional $49 (10/50,000, weekly), Premium
$159 (15/200,000, daily). No CDN cache analysis at all, so they are adjacent
rather than competing — but they show what a finished link checker looks like.

What we already have: link discovery during a crawl (page.go pulls <a href>,
assets and stylesheets), storage with found_on referrer tracking, an HTTP
checker with HEAD then GET fallback, redirect following, SSL and timeout
errors, and bot-block classification that is broader than theirs — we handle
454/455 from Danish hosts, they only list 403/429/430/503/999.

What is missing, cheapest first:

1. Blocked as a visible state. We classify bot-blocks correctly and then hide
   them, so a report says "1 broken" where theirs says "1 broken, 8 blocked".
   The customer cannot see that a link was skipped or why. Their wording is
   worth copying: "the link will probably work when you check it out in your
   browser."

2. Scheduled checks. Nothing runs the checker automatically — it only runs
   when someone clicks, and the button also refuses anything checked in the
   last 24 hours, which is not obvious from the UI. Dearbaby sat with 1,000
   unchecked links. This is the whole value of drlinkcheck's paid tiers.

3. Soft-404 detection. We check status codes only, so a WordPress site serving
   a 200 "nothing found" page passes clean. Their "large database of content
   patterns" is a regex list — For sale, Ads only, Placeholder, Out of
   service, No content, Directory listing, Error message are mostly
   domain-parking and CMS defaults. Needs response bodies, so HEAD is no
   longer enough.

4. Named result categories. We store a status code and an error string; they
   report about twenty named outcomes (Host not found, Connect error, SSL
   handshake error, SSL certificate problem, Too many redirects, Crawler trap,
   Bad content encoding, MX record not found). Mostly relabelling what we
   already detect.

5. Blacklist checks. Google Safe Browsing and PhishTank are both free. This is
   their paid hook and it is cheap.

6. Standalone crawl mode. The real blocker. Link discovery is currently a side
   effect of a cache crawl — RecordOutboundLinks fires from worker/pool.go
   inside a round. A link-check product must crawl a site with no
   extract_data, no cache headers and no package tier. That means a second
   crawl mode sharing the fetcher and queue while skipping everything
   cache-related, and discovery mode is already flagged untested with a
   channel-close race past 1024 queued pages.

Recommendation: build 1-5 inside CacheCrawler before splitting anything out.
They are worth having for existing customers regardless, and they are what a
separate product would need anyway. Real usage then answers whether anyone
wants it standalone, and by then only item 6 is left. Competing head-on with
an established single-purpose tool means competing on their SEO; bundling
means selling to people already paying us.

Starting with 1 and 2.

