Database backups — decided, not built (2026-09-11)

There are no backups of the dashboard database. Losing it ends the business:
users, sites, packages, invitations and support tickets all live there. Mongo
holds crawl history, which is embarrassing to lose but recoverable by
re-crawling, so it is a separate and lesser decision.

The database is about 1.3 MB. Daily backups with 90 days retention is roughly
120 MB, so size rules nothing out.

Where it landed after working through the options:

Google Drive works and is free, but its OAuth refresh token expires — after
seven days if the consent screen is left in Testing, which is the default and
easy to miss — and a backup job that stops silently is worse than none. A
service account avoids the token entirely and would be the way to do it if
Drive is chosen.

Backblaze B2 was the pick: 10 GB free permanently, no card at signup, and
S3-compatible so Laravel's built-in s3 driver works and the target can be
swapped by env var later. Blocked: their signup emails never arrived.

Cloudflare R2 is equivalent and also free at this size, but sources disagree
about whether enabling it needs a card on file.

Chosen direction: push backups over HTTP to a receiver endpoint on another
Laravel app on a different VPS. Genuinely offsite, free, no vendor account.
More code than an SFTP user and a larger attack surface, but nothing to renew
and nothing that expires.

Conditions, all of which matter more than the transport:

  - Encrypt the zip. The receiver is infrastructure that exists for something
    else and should hold a blob it cannot read.
  - Store outside the web root. A backup zip reachable by URL is the whole
    customer database, downloadable.
  - hash_equals for the token, not ===.
  - Rate limit and a size cap, so a leaked token cannot fill someone's disk.
  - HTTPS only.
  - Back up to local disk as well, so a receiver outage does not mean no
    backup at all.
  - Prove it by restoring into a scratch database. A backup nobody has
    restored is a guess.

spatie/laravel-backup does the dump, zip, encryption, retention and a
health check that alerts when the newest backup is missing or too small. The
crawler_scheduler container already runs, so there is nothing new to host.

Paused at the user's request. Nothing built yet.
